2025-06-02 19:32:38,974 - multi_agent_system.agent.11 - INFO - [execute_with_tools] Starting execution with command: {"task_description": "hi, how to call api using spark", "requirements": {"min_accuracy": 0.85, "max_time": 300, "output_format": "text", "context": {"user_id": "user-1", "session_id": "default-session", "conversation_history": []}}, "conversation_context": [{"agent_id": 10, "response": "Apache Spark itself doesn't have built-in support for making API calls. However, you can make API calls within Spark transformations using any HTTP client in the language you're programming with.\n\nHere's a basic example of how you might do this in Python using the `requests` library:\n\n```python\nimport requests\nfrom pyspark.sql import SparkSession\n\nspark = SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate()\ndata = [(\"value1\",), (\"value2\",), (\"value3\",)]\ndf = spark.createDataFrame(data, [\"Column\"])\n\ndef make_api_call(value):\n    response = requests.get(f\"http://my-api.com/endpoint?param={value}\")\n    return response.json()\n\ndf.rdd.map(lambda row: make_api_call(row[\"Column\"])).collect()\n\n```\n\nIn this example, `make_api_call` is a function that makes an API call for each row in the DataFrame.\n\nPlease note, if the API rate limits or has high latency, this can become a bottleneck for Spark job. You may want to include error handling and retry logic in the `make_api_call` function.\n\nRemember to avoid actions that will collect the data to the driver node when working with big datasets, as this can cause an out-of-memory error. Actions like `collect()` or `toPandas()` can cause such issues."}], "accuracy_threshold": 0.8, "success_rate": 0.9}
